WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.405
Welcome to Module 2 of the
Attribute-Driven Migration learning path:

2
00:00:04.405 --> 00:00:06.238
write a validation kit check.

3
00:00:06.238 --> 00:00:10.197
Every gate in the implementation
guide ends with a Validate step,

4
00:00:10.197 --> 00:00:12.837
and most of them name
a validation kit check.

5
00:00:12.837 --> 00:00:15.900
The kit is the executable
form of those steps.

6
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:21.656
This module serves Gate 3, and takes
Chapter 03 of Building the Attribute-Driven

7
00:00:21.656 --> 00:00:24.447
Migration, which teaches how a check is

8
00:00:24.447 --> 00:00:27.412
built through one new
check, V K SYNC oh two.

9
00:00:27.412 --> 00:00:31.270
It asks whether soft match and
hard-match takeover are blocked,

10
00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:34.088
and if not, whether an
approved window says why.

11
00:00:34.088 --> 00:00:38.725
You'll see the parts of a check, its three
severities, why a missing value reads

12
00:00:38.725 --> 00:00:43.362
as off, and why a read-only check still
has to ask for the least privilege it can.

13
00:00:43.362 --> 00:00:48.024
Then you'll run it on the kit's fixtures
and on an export, and break it on purpose.

14
00:00:48.024 --> 00:00:49.600
Nothing signs in.

15
00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:54.338
Why this check?

16
00:00:54.338 --> 00:00:57.200
Gate 3 maps attributes through sync.

17
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.731
Before a single AD user is synchronized
into a tenant that already has cloud

18
00:01:01.731 --> 00:01:04.392
accounts, the sync engine
has to decide which

19
00:01:04.392 --> 00:01:07.125
incoming object matches
which cloud account.

20
00:01:07.125 --> 00:01:11.455
Microsoft Learn's pages on configuring
Entra Connect for an existing

21
00:01:11.455 --> 00:01:15.262
tenant and on Set Entra Dir Sync
Feature explain the two joins.

22
00:01:15.262 --> 00:01:18.834
A soft match joins on
the primary SMTP address,

23
00:01:18.834 --> 00:01:22.499
and on the U P N when soft
match on U P N is turned on.

24
00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:24.875
A hard match joins on the source anchor.

25
00:01:24.875 --> 00:01:29.737
Either one, left open, lets an AD
object take over a cloud account.

26
00:01:29.737 --> 00:01:33.611
Learn's existing-tenant page
advises keeping soft match blocked,

27
00:01:33.611 --> 00:01:37.627
offers a feature to block cloud
object takeover through hard match,

28
00:01:37.627 --> 00:01:41.500
and says to turn each off only
while a matching procedure needs it.

29
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.391
So the question is simple:
are both blocks on,

30
00:01:44.391 --> 00:01:47.763
and if not, is there an
approved window that says why?

31
00:01:47.763 --> 00:01:52.060
The guide lists this as Gate 3's
optional match-blocking check.

32
00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:58.625
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

33
00:01:58.625 --> 00:02:01.248
which takes about forty-five
minutes with the lab.

34
00:02:01.248 --> 00:02:04.670
You'll describe the parts of
a check: its catalog entry,

35
00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:08.559
its function, its rows and outcome,
and its fixtures and tests.

36
00:02:08.559 --> 00:02:11.654
You'll explain the three
severities, Finding,

37
00:02:11.654 --> 00:02:14.998
Warn and Info, and why a
missing value reads as off.

38
00:02:14.998 --> 00:02:19.973
You'll explain why this check is optional
and prefers an export to a live read.

39
00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:24.985
You'll run it on fixtures and on an
export, and see Pass, Fail and Warn.

40
00:02:24.985 --> 00:02:29.772
And you'll explain what the safety tests
refuse, and why a new check inherits them.

41
00:02:29.772 --> 00:02:31.845
Before you start, finish Module 1,

42
00:02:31.845 --> 00:02:35.760
and Microsoft Automation Module
1 for permissions and roles.

43
00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.683
You need PowerShell 7
and Pester 5.5 or later,

44
00:02:38.683 --> 00:02:43.990
and you should be able to read PowerShell
and know Pester's Describe and It.

45
00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.240
This slide is the kit's own
design, not a Microsoft rule.

46
00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.490
In the kit, every check
has the same five parts.

47
00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:59.725
The catalog entry holds its I D, track,
title, source, Graph permissions,

48
00:02:59.725 --> 00:03:04.777
delegated role and release stage,
and marks it optional or preview.

49
00:03:04.777 --> 00:03:09.177
The function is named from the I
D, here Invoke V K Sync oh two,

50
00:03:09.177 --> 00:03:11.927
and wraps its body in a shared helper.

51
00:03:11.927 --> 00:03:15.583
The data read goes through
one path, for fixtures,

52
00:03:15.583 --> 00:03:19.603
Graph and input files, and
logs every read as evidence.

53
00:03:19.603 --> 00:03:23.065
The rows have one shape, and
the status comes from the rows.

54
00:03:23.065 --> 00:03:25.923
And the tests: the check
must pass clean data

55
00:03:25.923 --> 00:03:28.940
and fail planted problems
with an exact count.

56
00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:31.765
The wrapper does the work
no check should repeat.

57
00:03:31.765 --> 00:03:34.936
It catches any exception
and turns it into Error,

58
00:03:34.936 --> 00:03:38.028
so a read that throws can
never come back as Pass.

59
00:03:38.028 --> 00:03:41.988
Don't catch exceptions inside the
body to make a check more robust;

60
00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:44.560
that's how a check passes while blind.

61
00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:50.360
Here's the lesson this check hides.

62
00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:54.010
The read is GET directory,
on-premises synchronization.

63
00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.750
Microsoft Learn's Graph
reference for that call,

64
00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:01.545
Get on-premises directory synchronization,
names its least-privileged permission,

65
00:04:01.545 --> 00:04:05.723
OnPremDirectorySynchronization dot
Read dot All, which looks harmless.

66
00:04:05.723 --> 00:04:09.418
But the same page lists no
application permission for it at all,

67
00:04:09.418 --> 00:04:14.109
and for delegated access says Global
Administrator is the only supported role.

68
00:04:14.109 --> 00:04:18.998
A read-only check, run live, would need
the most powerful role in the tenant.

69
00:04:18.998 --> 00:04:22.885
The kit's design rule is that a
check asks for the least privilege

70
00:04:22.885 --> 00:04:26.773
the question needs, and GET-only
is necessary but not sufficient.

71
00:04:26.773 --> 00:04:29.173
So this check does three
things differently.

72
00:04:29.173 --> 00:04:33.798
It's optional, so it stays out of a
default run and runs only when named.

73
00:04:33.798 --> 00:04:37.223
It prefers an export, saved
by a Global Administrator

74
00:04:37.223 --> 00:04:39.948
who is already signed
in for other reasons.

75
00:04:39.948 --> 00:04:43.945
And it says so: the catalog
names the role and the reason,

76
00:04:43.945 --> 00:04:46.160
before anyone runs it live.

77
00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:53.622
Three severities, three meanings,
and they're the kit's design.

78
00:04:53.622 --> 00:04:55.335
A Finding fails the check.

79
00:04:55.335 --> 00:04:58.731
A Warn needs a person to look,
but isn't a failure: here,

80
00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:02.289
a block that's off inside an
approved matching window,

81
00:05:02.289 --> 00:05:05.847
which the run's options record
as the window's last day.

82
00:05:05.847 --> 00:05:11.111
The day after, the same state is a Finding
again, without anyone editing anything.

83
00:05:11.111 --> 00:05:16.321
An Info row gives context and isn't counted:
soft match on U P N, which Microsoft

84
00:05:16.321 --> 00:05:21.698
Learn's Set Entra Dir Sync Feature page
says can't be turned off once it's enabled.

85
00:05:21.698 --> 00:05:25.553
The status comes from the rows:
Fail if there's any Finding,

86
00:05:25.553 --> 00:05:28.097
else Warn if there's any Warn, else Pass.

87
00:05:28.097 --> 00:05:32.010
And the most important rule: a
missing value is read as off.

88
00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:37.110
So a response shape the kit doesn't expect
produces a Finding, not a silent Pass.

89
00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:41.610
Default every "is the control
on" read to the unsafe answer.

90
00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:46.560
NotApplicable is for a tenant with
nothing to check, never for a failed read.

91
00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:52.860
The safety tests are the kit's own design.

92
00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:58.449
They parse every script in the kit and fail
the build on any state-changing command:

93
00:05:58.449 --> 00:06:01.901
write verbs, Invoke Rest
Method, Invoke Web Request,

94
00:06:01.901 --> 00:06:04.860
native programs, file
redirection, and more.

95
00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:08.421
Every Graph request must
carry a literal Method GET,

96
00:06:08.421 --> 00:06:10.823
with one exception pinned by a test.

97
00:06:10.823 --> 00:06:14.024
A next link that points at a
host other than the cloud's

98
00:06:14.024 --> 00:06:16.934
own Graph host is refused,
so a token never leaves.

99
00:06:16.934 --> 00:06:20.331
This check passes those tests
without any change to them,

100
00:06:20.331 --> 00:06:23.222
because it reads only
through the kit's data path.

101
00:06:23.222 --> 00:06:25.387
That's the point of a single data path:

102
00:06:25.387 --> 00:06:29.572
a new check inherits the safety
properties instead of re-proving them.

103
00:06:29.572 --> 00:06:32.734
If your check seems to
need its own HTTP call,

104
00:06:32.734 --> 00:06:35.809
it doesn't; add what it
needs to the data path.

105
00:06:35.809 --> 00:06:40.918
And the remediation, the command Microsoft
Learn's existing-tenant page gives to

106
00:06:40.918 --> 00:06:44.120
block soft match, is a write,
so it stays out of the kit,

107
00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:46.910
in the change record the finding opens.

108
00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:51.385
Try it.

109
00:06:51.385 --> 00:06:55.270
Lab 1 runs in the validation-kit
folder of the examples,

110
00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:57.860
in PowerShell 7, and nothing signs in.

111
00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:03.351
Step one imports the module and runs the
check against the fail fixture, a fictional

112
00:07:03.351 --> 00:07:08.285
tenant with both blocks off and soft match
on U P N on, as of October thirtieth.

113
00:07:08.285 --> 00:07:10.960
The status is Fail, with a count of two.

114
00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.907
The details show why:
soft match isn't blocked,

115
00:07:13.907 --> 00:07:17.810
and cloud object takeover
through hard match isn't blocked.

116
00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:19.447
Those are the two Findings.

117
00:07:19.447 --> 00:07:22.569
The third row is Info:
soft match on U P N is on,

118
00:07:22.569 --> 00:07:25.335
and it can't be turned off once enabled.

119
00:07:25.335 --> 00:07:27.647
It's context, so it isn't counted.

120
00:07:27.647 --> 00:07:31.207
Then the evidence: the source
is Graph, in fixture mode,

121
00:07:31.207 --> 00:07:35.736
and the query names the fixture file
and the URI a live run would call.

122
00:07:35.736 --> 00:07:40.540
A reviewer can tell from the evidence
alone which path a result came from.

123
00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:48.240
Step two opens a matching window
until October thirty-first.

124
00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.457
Run the same fail fixture on October
thirtieth, inside the window,

125
00:07:52.457 --> 00:07:57.065
and the status is Warn: the blocks are
off, but an approved window says why.

126
00:07:57.065 --> 00:08:01.528
Run it on November first, the day
after the window closes, and it's Fail.

127
00:08:01.528 --> 00:08:03.253
Nobody edited anything.

128
00:08:03.253 --> 00:08:07.840
The window is a date, so a Warn
can never quietly become permanent.

129
00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:10.390
Step three feeds the check an export.

130
00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:13.420
The first two commands make
an inputs folder with an

131
00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:16.802
export that blocks soft match
but not hard-match takeover.

132
00:08:16.802 --> 00:08:21.377
The status is Fail with a count of
one: only the hard-match block is off.

133
00:08:21.377 --> 00:08:26.440
And the evidence's source is now Input,
with the export's file name as the query.

134
00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:28.304
That's the path the check prefers,

135
00:08:28.304 --> 00:08:32.470
because reading a file never needs
the Global Administrator role.

136
00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:39.880
Step four shows what a live run
would ask for: one permission,

137
00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:44.511
OnPremDirectorySynchronization dot
Read dot All, and the delegated role,

138
00:08:44.511 --> 00:08:47.872
Global Administrator, the
only role Microsoft Learn's

139
00:08:47.872 --> 00:08:51.308
Graph reference lists, with
no application permission.

140
00:08:51.308 --> 00:08:56.294
Step five confirms the check isn't in
a default run: the answer is False.

141
00:08:56.294 --> 00:09:01.758
Step six runs the whole kit's tests: two
hundred ninety-seven pass, none fail.

142
00:09:01.758 --> 00:09:04.770
Step seven makes the check
fail for the right reason.

143
00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:08.312
You change your copy of the
check to read block soft match,

144
00:09:08.312 --> 00:09:10.120
a property that doesn't exist.

145
00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.020
The fail fixture still fails.

146
00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:14.823
And the pass fixture, with both blocks on,

147
00:09:14.823 --> 00:09:18.220
fails too, because the
missing value reads as off.

148
00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:20.776
That's the unsafe default doing its job:

149
00:09:20.776 --> 00:09:23.970
a wrong property name can't
produce a silent Pass.

150
00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:27.158
Put the name back, and the
pass fixture passes again.

151
00:09:27.158 --> 00:09:30.110
Step eight removes the inputs folder.

152
00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:36.295
Lab 2 runs the check on a real export,

153
00:09:36.295 --> 00:09:41.248
in a test tenant with Microsoft Entra
Connect or Cloud Sync configured.

154
00:09:41.248 --> 00:09:44.342
A Global Administrator
of the test tenant saves

155
00:09:44.342 --> 00:09:47.436
the response of the one
privileged read to a file.

156
00:09:47.436 --> 00:09:51.573
That's the only step that needs the
role; nothing in the kit runs with it.

157
00:09:51.573 --> 00:09:56.397
Then you run the check on that export as
any user, and compare the result with the

158
00:09:56.397 --> 00:10:01.222
export, line by line, so you know the check
reads the tenant's real response shape.

159
00:10:01.222 --> 00:10:05.811
To back it out, step four deletes the
export once the run's evidence is stored.

160
00:10:05.811 --> 00:10:10.107
The export isn't evidence by
itself; the run's manifest,

161
00:10:10.107 --> 00:10:13.135
with a hash for every file of the run, is.

162
00:10:13.135 --> 00:10:15.285
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

163
00:10:15.285 --> 00:10:19.130
Follow Chapter 03's Lab 2, step by step.

164
00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:24.267
The federal requirement.

165
00:10:24.267 --> 00:10:32.549
The controls are NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three controls in its

166
00:10:32.549 --> 00:10:36.981
Moderate baseline: C M six,
C A seven, A C two, A C six,

167
00:10:36.981 --> 00:10:42.230
S A eleven, C M seven, S I seven,
A U three, C M three and C M five.

168
00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:46.244
A check is monitoring evidence only
if it can't change what it reads,

169
00:10:46.244 --> 00:10:48.142
and can't pass when it didn't look.

170
00:10:48.142 --> 00:10:50.955
The run's validation
results show that an AD

171
00:10:50.955 --> 00:10:54.080
object can't take over a
cloud account unnoticed.

172
00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:57.510
A deviation is approved,
documented, and has an end:

173
00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:01.442
the window date in the run's
options, and its change record.

174
00:11:01.442 --> 00:11:06.526
Least privilege: no standing Global
Administrator identity for a scheduled check,

175
00:11:06.526 --> 00:11:10.504
shown by the evidence block that
says the read came from an export.

176
00:11:10.504 --> 00:11:15.130
The test results, including the safety
tests, show the kit can only read.

177
00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:20.430
In Lab 2, the validation manifest
holds a hash for every file of the run.

178
00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:24.693
And the remediation is a write under
change control, never the check.

179
00:11:24.693 --> 00:11:29.603
The Key Security Indicators come from
FedRAMP's Key Security Indicators list,

180
00:11:29.603 --> 00:11:33.229
FedRAMP 20x indicators for
cloud service providers.

181
00:11:33.229 --> 00:11:37.150
Which output supports which
control is this workbook's reading.

182
00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:42.875
Check yourself with two questions.

183
00:11:42.875 --> 00:11:45.852
First: the fixture says
soft match isn't blocked,

184
00:11:45.852 --> 00:11:48.600
and an approved matching
window ends today.

185
00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:51.725
What does the check report
tomorrow, if nothing changes?

186
00:11:51.725 --> 00:11:53.700
The answer is b: Fail.

187
00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:58.200
The day after the window's last day,
the same state is a Finding again.

188
00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.312
You saw that in step two of the lab.

189
00:12:00.312 --> 00:12:05.099
Second: a response comes back without
the block soft match enabled property.

190
00:12:05.099 --> 00:12:06.513
What does the check report?

191
00:12:06.513 --> 00:12:11.151
The answer is b: a Finding, because
a missing value reads as off.

192
00:12:11.151 --> 00:12:14.525
You saw that in step seven,
when the pass fixture failed.

193
00:12:14.525 --> 00:12:19.449
The module page has two more questions,
on why the check is left out of a default

194
00:12:19.449 --> 00:12:24.810
run and where a new HTTP call belongs, each
linked to the section that answers it.

195
00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:29.385
To sum up.

196
00:12:29.385 --> 00:12:33.949
You built your understanding of a check
from its catalog entry to its safety tests,

197
00:12:33.949 --> 00:12:36.700
and saw how one check asks
for the least privilege

198
00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:39.385
it can when its read needs
the most powerful role.

199
00:12:39.385 --> 00:12:43.034
The evidence an ISSO keeps:
the run's results, details,

200
00:12:43.034 --> 00:12:46.938
summary and validation
manifest, in the evidence store;

201
00:12:46.938 --> 00:12:52.624
the window date and its change record; the
Entra sign-in log for the one privileged

202
00:12:52.624 --> 00:12:58.310
export; and the change record for any
remediation, which is never made by the check.

203
00:12:58.310 --> 00:13:02.735
Before shipping a new check, work
through the Chapter 03 checklist.

204
00:13:02.735 --> 00:13:06.485
Next is Module 3: keep
dynamic rules as code.

205
00:13:06.485 --> 00:13:12.135
A dynamic rule is access policy, and one
edit can remove hundreds of people at once.

206
00:13:12.135 --> 00:13:16.184
You'll keep each rule as a reviewed
file with tests and a linter,

207
00:13:16.184 --> 00:13:21.240
see whom a change adds and removes, and
pause and resume safely in an incident.
